ood sense of what these items  are and it's interesting to plow forward  for example I tweeted out yesterday that  yesterday I read and listened although I  didn't understand fully a TED talk by a  young Turkish woman who had cancer and  had her leg amputated a description of  the sustain iam dynasty or dynasty in  ancient Persia from person online and a  discussion on cosmic at about the hijab  and secularism and Islam in French  society all three subject very  interesting so basically I'm interested  in the subject matter I'm plowing  forward saving new words not learning  them but just saving them for later  and I am kind of reading and getting  some overview of the language because  okay so that so that's so I'm doing  things that are more interesting I'm  forcing myself to move forward get out  of my comfort zone I'm making sure that  I put enough time into each language so  another question was how long does it  take so I tied myself this morning  obviously Turkish is the fastest because  it's written in the Latin alphabet so I  can read faster also I have them I have  typically a higher percentage of new  words in Turkish for whatever reason  then in the other two languages so to  get to a hundred links in Turkish took  me about 15 minutes in Persian and  Arabic it takes 30 minutes so that means  that an hour and 15 minutes an hour and  a half a day  has to be spent on creating these links  in the three different languages so if  you are only doing one language you can  still force yourself to do 300 links a  day like I'm doing and it should take  you an hour and a half okay you're  you're not trying to remember anything  you're not trying to remember nothing  new words that you looked up nor the  other yellow words that you're reading  through at the same time in which you  still can't remember but you look them  up again none of that matters what I'm  finding is another advantage of power  language learning like I'm doing is that  it increases your ability 